## One suggestion I'd make

I would introduce a **Rule Engine** between the Knowledge Base and the SEO Agent.

```text
Knowledge Base
        │
        ▼
Rule Engine
        │
        ▼
SEO Agent
```

The Knowledge Base stores **information** (documentation, examples, guidelines, patterns).

The Rule Engine converts that information into **machine-executable rules**, for example:

* "Every page must have a unique title."
* "Every indexable page must have a canonical URL."
* "Images should include meaningful alt text."
* "Structured data must validate without errors."

This keeps the SEO Agent focused on **reasoning and implementation**, while the Rule Engine provides the standards it must satisfy. It also makes updating the system easier—when Google guidance changes, you update rules rather than changing agent behavior.

I think this architecture will scale much better as you later add AEO, GEO, accessibility, performance, security, and other engineering skill packs. The planner, critic, verifier, MCP server, and orchestration loop remain the same; only the domain-specific rules, knowledge, and tools change.
Yes, and I think this is where we should separate the responsibilities very clearly.

